1
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,840
And now the kung pao chicken.

2
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:11,880
Smooth.

3
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,380
And finally,

4
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:15,670
my moo shu pork.

5
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:19,690
Whoo-hoo!

6
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:21,210
Oh, there you have it, gentlemen.

7
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:24,510
Our entire dinner unpacked by robot.

8
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,030
And it only took 28 minutes.

9
00:00:28,050 --> 00:00:30,960
Impressive, but we must be cautious.

10
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:34,930
- Why?  - Today, it's a Chinese food retrieval robot.

11
00:00:34,930 --> 00:00:37,050
Tomorrow, it travels back in time

12
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:39,250
and tries to kill Sarah Connor.

13
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:41,980
I don't think that's going to happen, Sheldon.

14
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:43,560
No one ever does.

15
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:45,480
That's why it happens.

16
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:48,000
Hey. Is the food here?

17
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,450
Ooh. What's that?

18
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:50,580
That, dear lady,

19
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:52,950
is the Wolowitz Programmable Hand,

20
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:54,720
designed for extravehicular repairs

21
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:57,070
on the International Space Station.

22
00:00:57,070 --> 00:00:58,560
Ah, cool.

23
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:00,320
Ask me to pass the soy sauce.

24
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:03,040
Oh, does that come up much on the space station?

25
00:01:03,490 --> 00:01:06,310
Mostly with Asian and Jewish astronauts.

26
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:09,520
- All right. Pass the soy sauce.  - Coming up.

27
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:22,370
So how's work?

28
00:01:22,370 --> 00:01:23,810
Oh, it's not bad.

29
00:01:23,810 --> 00:01:24,930
Kind of hungry.

30
00:01:24,930 --> 00:01:26,570
Yeah, we all are.

31
00:01:26,990 --> 00:01:28,390
Just wait.

32
00:01:28,390 --> 00:01:30,330
You realize, Penny, that the technology

33
00:01:30,330 --> 00:01:32,230
that went into this arm will one day make

34
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:35,890
unskilled food servers such as yourself obsolete.

35
00:01:36,540 --> 00:01:37,140
Really?

36
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:39,730
They're going to make a robot that spits on your hamburger?

37
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:43,270
I thought you broke up with her.

38
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:44,820
Why is she here?

39
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:46,940
Okay, here we go.

40
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:49,470
Passing the soy sauce.

41
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:52,930
Put out your hand.

42
00:01:58,350 --> 00:01:59,430
That's amazing.

43
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:01,290
I wouldn't say amazing.

44
00:02:01,290 --> 00:02:03,210
At best, it's a modest leap forward

45
00:02:03,210 --> 00:02:07,580
from the basic technology that gave us Country Bear Jamboree.

46
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:09,790
Hey, Sheldon?

47
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:11,060
Yes?

48
00:02:19,030 --> 00:02:20,280
Peace?

49
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:21,690
No, not peace.

50
00:02:21,690 --> 00:02:22,340
Hang on.

51
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:53,130
Does NASA know you're using that thing as a napkin holder?

52
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:56,720
You kidding? They still think it's in a secure locker at JPL.

53
00:02:57,810 --> 00:02:59,240
You stole it?

54
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:00,340
Borrowed.

55
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:03,220
The trick is to carry it out to your car like you own it.

56
00:03:05,410 --> 00:03:06,890
Excuse me.

57
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:11,230
Amy's at the dry cleaners,

58
00:03:11,230 --> 00:03:13,830
and she's made a very amusing pun.

59
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:16,560
"I don't care for perchloroethylene,

60
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:19,700
and I don't like glycol ether."

61
00:03:24,980 --> 00:03:25,660
Get it?

62
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:29,060
She doesn't like glycol ether.

63
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:33,090
Sounds like "either."

64
00:03:37,670 --> 00:03:39,840
"L-O-L."

65
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:42,310
- Who's Amy?  - His girlfriend.

66
00:03:42,310 --> 00:03:44,450
- Sheldon has a girlfriend?  - She's not my girlfriend.

67
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:46,280
- How long has this been going on?  - Four months.

68
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:47,450
She's not my girlfriend.

69
00:03:47,450 --> 00:03:48,910
Are you telling me, for the past four months

70
00:03:48,910 --> 00:03:50,450
I have been asking you, "What's new?"

71
00:03:50,450 --> 00:03:51,530
and you never thought to go with,

72
00:03:51,530 --> 00:03:53,170
"Sheldon has a girlfriend"?

73
00:03:53,170 --> 00:03:54,700
She's not my girlfriend.

74
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:55,790
Ah... d-d-d-d...

75
00:03:55,790 --> 00:03:57,240
How did they meet?

76
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:59,700
Raj and I entered Sheldon's information on a dating site,

77
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:01,780
and it spit out Amy Farrah Fowler.

78
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:04,380
Oh, my God! Sheldon and Amy.

79
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:06,250
Or, as we call them, "Shamy."

80
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:08,990
Shamy.

81
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:10,880
I am so digging the Shamy.

82
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:13,540
All right, everyone pay attention.

83
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:16,390
Yes, I have a friend named Amy Farrah Fowler.

84
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:17,800
Yes, she is female.

85
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:20,100
Yes, we communicate on a daily basis,

86
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:22,810
but no, she is not my girlfriend.

87
00:04:23,210 --> 00:04:25,570
Okay, well, what do you communicate about?

88
00:04:25,570 --> 00:04:29,040
Well, my work in physics, her work in neurobiology,

89
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:33,050
and most recently, the possibility of our having a child together.

90
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:42,280
Thank you.

91
00:04:43,430 --> 00:04:45,050
Wait a minute-- a child?

92
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:46,130
You never see this girl.

93
00:04:46,130 --> 00:04:48,270
You just e-mail and text and Twitter.

94
00:04:48,270 --> 00:04:50,460
Now you're considering having a baby?

95
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:52,920
Amy pointed out that between the two of us,

96
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:55,000
our genetic material has the potential

97
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:56,880
of producing the first in a line

98
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:59,670
of intellectually superior, benign overlords

99
00:04:59,670 --> 00:05:02,600
to guide humanity to a brighter tomorrow.

100
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:06,570
I'm guessing that future historians

101
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:10,770
will condemn us for not taking this opportunity to kill Sheldon.

102
00:05:11,880 --> 00:05:14,030
Okay, I have a question.

103
00:05:14,030 --> 00:05:14,960
Yes, Penny.

104
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:17,010
You don't even like people touching you.

105
00:05:17,010 --> 00:05:18,840
How are you going to have sex?

106
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:22,940
Why on earth would we have sex?

107
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:27,050
Oh, honey, did your mom not have the talk with you?

108
00:05:27,050 --> 00:05:30,460
You know, when your private parts started growing?

109
00:05:30,730 --> 00:05:33,700
I'm quite aware of the way humans usually reproduce,

110
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:35,760
Which is messy, unsanitary,

111
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:37,920
And based on living next to you for three years,

112
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:41,480
Involves loud and unnecessary appeals to a deity.

113
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:43,130
Oh, God.

114
00:05:43,130 --> 00:05:44,830
Yes, exactly.

115
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:50,060
Consequently, if Amy and I choose

116
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:51,700
to bring new life into this world,

117
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:53,540
It will be accomplished clinically,

118
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:56,670
with fertility experts in a lab with petri dishes.

119
00:05:56,670 --> 00:05:57,700
Which reminds me--

120
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:00,730
you have broad hips and a certain corn-fed vigor.

121
00:06:00,730 --> 00:06:03,680
Is your womb available for rental?

122
00:06:05,610 --> 00:06:07,920
Still digging the Shamy?

123
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:10,120
Look, Sheldon,

124
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:12,200
before you race off to the fertility clinic,

125
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:13,410
you might want to think about--

126
00:06:13,410 --> 00:06:14,490
uh, gee, I don't know--

127
00:06:14,490 --> 00:06:17,280
maybe actually spending some time with her.

128
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:19,280
- You mean dating?  - Yeah.

129
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:20,860
- I can't date Amy.  - Why not?

130
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:22,830
She's not my girlfriend.

131
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:24,980
Okay, look, don't think of it as dating a girlfriend.

132
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:26,300
Think of it as, uh...

133
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:28,930
Getting to know the future mother of your child.

134
00:06:30,870 --> 00:06:33,260
I hadn't considered that.

135
00:06:33,260 --> 00:06:35,900
I suppose she will have to have access to our progeny.

136
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:37,480
And you don't think I can achieve

137
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:40,710
the required intimacy via text messaging?

138
00:06:40,710 --> 00:06:42,020
Probably not.

139
00:06:43,130 --> 00:06:46,850
It would appear as if the phone companies have been lying to me.

140
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:51,420
Penny.

141
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:53,710
Penny. Penny.

142
00:06:55,070 --> 00:06:57,390
You do realize I stand on the other side of the door

143
00:06:57,390 --> 00:07:00,210
waiting for you to finish knocking three times.

144
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:03,820
I know. I can see the shadow of your feet under the door.

145
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:06,800
Yeah, my point is it's a waste of time.

146
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:08,110
If you're looking for an example

147
00:07:08,110 --> 00:07:09,820
of a waste of time, I would refer you

148
00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:12,050
to the conversation we're having right now.

149
00:07:12,510 --> 00:07:13,280
What do you want?

150
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:15,940
I've decided to take your advice and have arranged

151
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:18,190
to go on a date with Amy Farrah Fowler.

152
00:07:18,190 --> 00:07:19,230
Oh, that's great.

153
00:07:19,230 --> 00:07:21,080
- Have fun.  - Wait.

154
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:21,960
You have to drive me.

155
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:24,080
- What?  - You know I don't drive.

156
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:25,920
- Well, go ask Leonard.  - I did.

157
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:28,250
He said-- and I quote-- "Ask Penny.

158
00:07:28,250 --> 00:07:30,440
It was her cockamamy idea."

159
00:07:31,650 --> 00:07:33,030
Leonard said "cockamamy"?

160
00:07:33,030 --> 00:07:34,940
Actually, I'm paraphrasing.

161
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:36,630
Having been raised in a Christian household,

162
00:07:36,630 --> 00:07:39,270
I'm uncomfortable with the language he used.

163
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:40,940
And to be honest,

164
00:07:40,940 --> 00:07:43,620
I'm not entirely comfortable with "cockamamy."

165
00:07:44,260 --> 00:07:45,740
Okay, fine. When's the date?

166
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:47,410
- Now.  - Now?

167
00:07:47,410 --> 00:07:49,190
Hurry. We're going to be late.

168
00:07:50,670 --> 00:07:52,010
Sheldon, did it ever occur to you

169
00:07:52,010 --> 00:07:54,200
that I might have other plans?

170
00:07:54,200 --> 00:07:55,170
I'm sorry.

171
00:07:55,170 --> 00:07:57,380
Do you have other plans?

172
00:08:00,130 --> 00:08:03,420
Well, no, not per se, but...

173
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:05,460
So this conversation is as pointless

174
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:08,000
as your door-knocking soliloquy?

175
00:08:09,390 --> 00:08:12,560
Let me get my... cockamamy keys.

176
00:08:15,210 --> 00:08:17,740
Oh, God, that feels so good.

177
00:08:23,170 --> 00:08:24,150
Yeah, that's the spot.

178
00:08:24,150 --> 00:08:25,910
Oh, baby.

179
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:29,830
Howard, dinner's ready!

180
00:08:29,830 --> 00:08:30,740
I'll eat later.

181
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:32,470
I'm busy!

182
00:08:33,540 --> 00:08:34,370
Oh, yeah.

183
00:08:34,370 --> 00:08:36,410
Just like a real hand.

184
00:08:49,530 --> 00:08:51,230
Thank you for driving me.

185
00:08:51,230 --> 00:08:52,750
You're welcome.

186
00:08:54,160 --> 00:08:56,750
I wish you weren't wearing flip-flops.

187
00:08:56,750 --> 00:08:58,970
It's dangerous to drive in flip-flops.

188
00:08:58,970 --> 00:09:00,580
- Sheldon.  - Sorry.

189
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:04,790
I just don't want to be yet another flip-flop fatality.

190
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:09,180
Can I ask you a question?

191
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:11,080
Given your community college education,

192
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:14,360
I encourage you to ask me as many as possible.

193
00:09:15,470 --> 00:09:17,430
Yeah. Well, my question is--

194
00:09:17,430 --> 00:09:20,090
and I'm pretty sure I know the answer--

195
00:09:20,090 --> 00:09:22,000
Is this your first date?

196
00:09:22,410 --> 00:09:24,060
That depends.

197
00:09:24,060 --> 00:09:26,140
Does square-dancing with my sister

198
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:28,780
at a Teens for Jesus Fourth of July Hoedown

199
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:30,320
count as a date?

200
00:09:30,780 --> 00:09:31,590
No.

201
00:09:31,590 --> 00:09:33,500
Then, this is my first date.

202
00:09:34,890 --> 00:09:36,610
Okay. Well, then, there's a couple of things

203
00:09:36,610 --> 00:09:38,480
you should probably know.

204
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:41,400
I have a master's degree and two doctorates.

205
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:44,470
The things I should know, I do know.

206
00:09:45,750 --> 00:09:48,910
My point is, I know more about dating than you,

207
00:09:48,910 --> 00:09:50,590
and if you were as smart as you think you are,

208
00:09:50,590 --> 00:09:52,220
you would listen to me.

209
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:56,100
If you know so much, how come I have a date tonight

210
00:09:56,100 --> 00:09:59,310
and you have nothing better to do than drive me to it?

211
00:10:02,090 --> 00:10:03,760
Fair point.

212
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:09,880
You know, there's something I've always wondered about Aquaman.

213
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:12,250
- Yeah?  - Where does he poop?

214
00:10:15,090 --> 00:10:15,950
What?

215
00:10:15,950 --> 00:10:18,610
What would a toilet look like in Atlantis?

216
00:10:18,610 --> 00:10:19,540
How would you flush it?

217
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:23,420
And when you did flush it, where would the poop go?

218
00:10:27,230 --> 00:10:28,910
Hold that thought.

219
00:10:29,850 --> 00:10:31,660
Hey, Howard. What's going on?

220
00:10:32,450 --> 00:10:33,830
What...

221
00:10:33,830 --> 00:10:36,240
Hold on. Howard, Howard, slow down.

222
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:38,820
The robot hand is stuck on your what?

223
00:10:42,430 --> 00:10:44,570
You're not going to believe this.

224
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,930
So, um, Amy, Sheldon tells me

225
00:11:01,930 --> 00:11:05,080
you're a neuro... something-or-other.

226
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:08,030
Neurobiologist.

227
00:11:08,030 --> 00:11:10,470
Your "check engine" light is on.

228
00:11:12,350 --> 00:11:13,700
Yeah, it's okay.

229
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:14,710
But the light indicates...

230
00:11:14,710 --> 00:11:16,070
Don't bother.

231
00:11:16,070 --> 00:11:17,750
I've wasted many an hour

232
00:11:17,750 --> 00:11:20,420
tilting at that particular windmill.

233
00:11:22,830 --> 00:11:24,400
Uh, what is that scent you're wearing?

234
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:25,890
It smells great.

235
00:11:25,890 --> 00:11:27,880
Dandruff shampoo.

236
00:11:29,270 --> 00:11:31,650
I have dry scalp.

237
00:11:32,990 --> 00:11:35,610
Well, your hair looks very nice.

238
00:11:35,610 --> 00:11:38,150
Are you a homosexual?

239
00:11:38,930 --> 00:11:42,140
No, no, I'm just giving you a compliment.

240
00:11:43,620 --> 00:11:46,690
Would have been more flattered if you were a homosexual.

241
00:11:49,370 --> 00:11:50,480
Guys, how 'bout some music?

242
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:52,650
Oh, no, I wouldn't care for that.

243
00:11:52,650 --> 00:11:53,290
Amy?

244
00:11:53,290 --> 00:11:55,030
No, thank you.

245
00:11:55,030 --> 00:11:56,320
Okay.

246
00:11:56,320 --> 00:11:58,910
Uncomfortable silence it is.

247
00:12:03,140 --> 00:12:05,100
Hey, Sheldon, have you told Amy

248
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:07,900
what it was like for you growing up in Texas?

249
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:09,250
No.

250
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:13,570
Well, why don't you tell her?

251
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:15,390
All right.

252
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:17,510
It was hell.

253
00:12:19,430 --> 00:12:21,360
Any follow up, Amy?

254
00:12:22,350 --> 00:12:24,030
No.

255
00:12:28,530 --> 00:12:30,440
I myself grew up in Nebraska.

256
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:32,220
Small town outside of Omaha.

257
00:12:32,220 --> 00:12:37,370
Nice place, mostly family farms, a few meth labs.

258
00:12:37,370 --> 00:12:41,100
I'm sorry, how is this better than uncomfortable silence?

259
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:43,120
I don't know. I was just trying something.

260
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:47,550
Muggles.

261
00:12:53,670 --> 00:12:57,770
You slipped and fell into a robot hand?

262
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:01,110
Yes.

263
00:13:01,930 --> 00:13:03,850
Penis first?

264
00:13:07,110 --> 00:13:09,050
Yes. Now, help me!

265
00:13:09,050 --> 00:13:10,640
I'd suggest a lubricant, but I have a feeling

266
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:12,730
you fell on some of that as well.

267
00:13:13,970 --> 00:13:15,440
Not funny, Leonard.

268
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:18,330
Really? A robot hand's got a death grip on your junk, dude.

269
00:13:18,330 --> 00:13:20,220
That's funny, ask anyone.

270
00:13:21,260 --> 00:13:23,890
Plea-- before my mother walks in, get this off me!

271
00:13:23,890 --> 00:13:24,930
Okay, let's see.

272
00:13:24,930 --> 00:13:25,820
